Reference: Milroy 1999 Sec. III.A (lambda = 11.07, gamma = 14.9 / 16.6 / 18.2).
See COMPARISON.md for the full comparison and for what changed and why.

Current code
  rmf_solver.py       solver (2nd order in r, vectorised)
  run_milroy.py       the three reference cases -> all_results_corrected.pkl
  plot.py             figures from a results pickle
  validate_numerics.py  convergence + ablation study
  study_thresholds.py   gamma_c and the expulsion threshold (Milroy Figs. 4, 6, 9)

Production settings
  lambda = 11.07, Nr = 64, dt = 0.002 (1571 steps/period), 200 periods
  ~30 s per value of gamma
  dt = 0.003 is unstable, and the limit tightens as gamma grows (0.002 fails
  near gamma = 20); dt = 0.002 matches dt = 0.001 to five digits.
  gamma = 14.9 needs ~200 periods to reach its steady alpha_s; 101 is not enough.

The rise time
  The gamma(t) = gamma (1 - exp(-t/tau_r)) ramp is Hugrass & Grimm 1981 Eq. (3),
  so it is a real part of their model, but their tau_r = 0.4 us at omega = 5e6/s
  is omega*tau_r = 2.0, i.e. 0.32 of an RMF period, not 3 periods. Milroy drops
  it entirely. At 0.32 T it costs 0.4 T of penetration time; at 3 T it costs 6 T.
  rmf_solver.RISE_TIME_HG1981 holds their value; the default is 0 (Milroy).

Data
  all_results_corrected.pkl     current scheme, Nr=64, 200 periods
  all_results_legacy_Nr64.pkl   old scheme at the same Nr and duration, for the
                                before/after figure
  alpha_s_up.txt                ascending gamma sweep (Milroy Fig. 6 / Eq. 18)

Old code
  nr32_fix.py produced all_results_Nr32_6283steps.pkl and
  all_results_Nr64_6283steps_fine.pkl (dt = 0.001, gamma ramped over 3 periods).
